[6] WHICH TWO ENTRIES ARE RAISED -- the S2 sub-family vs the S1 sub-family
    r39's finding (draft Sec 7.54 e) is that WHICH pair of entries is raised, not how
    many copies of the maximum exist, is what excludes the counterexamples.  So the
    two sub-families must be told apart explicitly.
   S2-derived pair (L, L'_1): the two raised entries are BOTH copies of c-1, both in
   the head block.  Measured as the multiset difference in [2]: 686 of 686.
   S1 pair (L(T), L(T+e_i)): one raise is the head flip W-(c)->W+(c) (a c-1 -> c) and
   the OTHER is a tail part T_j -> T_j+1 : 2485 of 2485 S1 instances whose difference is a
   2-up/2-down multiset move.
   population: the 3057 S1 instances of block [1].
   ==> the two sub-families are DISJOINT in raise-position: S2 raises two entries at the
   SAME level (c-1, both in the head), S1 raises one in the head and one in the tail.
   Consequence stated plainly: the reduction does NOT put S2 inside C1-W's census
   population.  It puts it inside the SAME STATEMENT on an adjacent population.

